<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sergio Osmeña</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sergio Osmeña was a Filipino politician who, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-l-quezon">Manuel Quezon</a>, led the Nacionalista Party and campaigned for independence under American rule, helping to secure the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tydings-mcduffie-act">Tydings–McDuffie Act</a> of 1934. He became vice-president of the Commonwealth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in 1935 and president on Quezon's death in exile in 1944, returning with American forces to Leyte. He lost the 1946 election to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-roxas">Manuel Roxas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sergio-osmena.jpg" alt="Sergio Osmeña" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Library of the Philippines (distributed by Philippine Presidential Museum and Library) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sergio_Osmena_photo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
